How to judge:
- The page words things differently than the request does, and that is the normal case — an exact wording match has already been tried and failed. Judge what the value MEASURES, in the context of the page it is on.
- The page is showing the result of the query/filter in its URL. A generic label ("results found", "items", "total") on a page filtered to the requested subject IS the requested quantity — the filter is what makes it specific, not the label.
- A candidate is NOT the requested value when it measures a different KIND of thing: a percentage, share or rate when a count was asked for; a duration when a total was asked for; a different entity's quantity.
- Omit an output path when no candidate measures the requested quantity. An empty selections list is the right answer when the page does not show it at all — but do not abstain merely because the label uses the page's vocabulary instead of the request's.
- Never use a candidate_index outside the list, and never map two output paths to the same candidate.

